        'Three explicitly hypothetical100MW/400MWh storage assets in DE-LU,FR and ES submit charging-parent/discharging-child cycles, with90% charging and90% discharging efficiency. Charging is offered at06:00–07:00UTC and discharge at17:00–18:00UTC; gas packages cover16:00–19:00UTC. Initial and terminal storage energy are both constrained to zero every day. That is an explicit restrictive daily-cycle strategy, not an unaccounted reset or a claim to represent actual European battery/PHS capacity. Hydro remains fixed.','',
        'The accepted resource_full inputs, country solar factors, weather, demand, original MW, oil/coal/nuclear bids and grid limits are preserved. Storage bid limits use the retained **own-model** baseline-price forecast, not observed ENTSO-E prices. Loading that precomputed forecast is included; producing a fresh forecast solve is excluded. Historical source reference clearing took 51.8 s, but that is a different workload and not a timing measured by this new run. No price-target fitting or baseline/public-site promotion occurs.','',
        'All24 delivery hours are represented in each daily result. Six connected hours enter the coupled optimization;18 unchanged independent hours reuse the exact checked reference witness. Storage profiles are zero between the retained hours and standing loss is zero, so energy remains constant during omitted periods. This exact time-component shortcut does not apply if new bid profiles or physical constraints connect those omitted hours. The benchmark therefore measures this specified hourly UTC book, not arbitrary24-hour flexible schedules or a reproduction of2025 quarter-hour/local-DST exchange rules.','',
        '## Implementation and timing breakdown','',
        'Precompute the unchanged sparse topology once per process; refresh every daily offer cost, renewable/source bound, demand and network-flow RHS. Cold-versus-cached sample books produce identical objectives. Two cache tests cover changed costs/demand/capacity, profile-triggered rebuilding and stale-reservation rejection.','',
        'Allocation is a bounded MILP. Fixed-integer LP duals are checked directly against stationarity, duality, complementarity and accepted-family surplus. If those prices fail, search alternatives. An infeasible price search triggers heuristic rejection of the offending root package followed by complete network re-clearing; numerical failures/timeouts never become rejection cuts. Preserve the original unrestricted allocation bound, since later heuristic-cut bounds are not globally valid price-clearing bounds.','',
        f"{recleared} of {days} checked days need more than one allocation/pricing pass; maximum **{int(rounds.max())} passes**. The retained cost interval is an offer-cost/welfare bound for this prototype; it is **not observed-price error**, investment value or full EUPHEMIA optimality. Maximum remaining interval width is {max(r['conservative_cost_gap_eur'] for r in passed):.2f}EUR. Five cut-loop tests include exhaustive tiny-book comparison and a deliberately nonzero bound after rejecting a paradoxically accepted block; three direct-price tests preserve both rejection and profitable-child rescue.",'',
        '| Phase, completed days | Measured seconds |',
